EXT. STREET - DAY

Aanya, an autistic eight-year-old girl, walks down the sidewalk. She wears a mustard yellow sweater with the sleeves pulled over her hands. Around her neck rest gray noise-reducing headphones.

Aanya has one goal today. She wants to reach the quiet library courtyard. In her pocket, her fingers turn a smooth gray pebble. It is her totem. She needs to place it on the perfect corner of the stone bench.

Her sneakers step in a perfect rhythm. One, two, three, crack. One, two, three, crack. She avoids the sidewalk lines with practiced ease.

Priya, her mom, walks beside her. Priya carries a blue tote bag.

PRIYA
We have five minutes before the library closes its garden, Aanya. Ready?

Aanya does not look up, but she nods.

AANYA
Seventy-two more steps. Then the quiet bench.

EXT. OAK STREET CROSSING - CONTINUOUS

They reach the curb. The pedestrian signal glows with a white walking figure.

Aanya stops. She tilts her head.

The signal changes to an orange flashing hand. Usually, it blinks exactly four times. One. Two. Three. Four. Then it stays solid.

Today, the hand blinks. One. Two. Three. Solid.

Aanya frowns. She pulls her notebook from her sweater pocket.

AANYA
It missed a beat.

PRIYA
The light? It looks fine, sweetie. Come on, the sign says walk.

AANYA
It was three blinks. It is always four. The interval is short by one point two seconds.

Priya gently nudges her forward.

PRIYA
Maybe the bulb is just old. Let us get to your bench.

Aanya steps onto the street, but her eyes stay locked on the black metal signal box.

INT. AANYA'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The room is quiet and organized. Colored markers sit in a neat row by shade.

Aanya sits at her desk. She draws a grid of the town's intersections. Oak Street. Elm Street. Maple Street.

She turns the smooth gray pebble in her left hand. The cool stone helps her think.

She writes down the numbers.
Oak Street: 3 blinks.
Elm Street: 4 blinks.
Maple Street: 4 blinks.

AANYA
(to herself)
A pattern always has a rule. What is the rule?

She draws a line connecting the streets. The grid looks like a falling staircase.

EXT. ELM STREET CROSSING - NEXT DAY

The morning sun is bright. The street is busy.

Aanya stands at the curb with Toby, a non-disabled boy from her class. Toby bounces on his heels, playing with a yellow plastic yo-yo.

TOBY
Did you finish the math homework? It was so long.

Aanya does not look at the yo-yo. She is watching the Elm Street crossing signal.

The white walking figure disappears. The orange hand begins to flash.

One. Two. Solid.

Aanya gasps. She grabs her notebook.

AANYA
Two blinks. Yesterday it was four.

TOBY
What? The light? Who cares? It is green for us. Let us go!

Toby steps off the curb.

AANYA
Toby, stop!

Aanya grabs his backpack and pulls him back.

A car speeds through the yellow light, rushing past the crosswalk. Toby blinks, startled.

TOBY
Whoa. That was close.

AANYA
The light changed too fast because the sequence is broken. The gap is shrinking by one point two seconds every twelve hours.

Toby looks at the signal, then at Aanya. He shrugs.

TOBY
It is just a glitchy light, Aanya. The city will fix it eventually.

AANYA
They do not know the rule. If they do not know the rule, they cannot fix it before the big one goes.

EXT. SCHOOL CROSSING - DAY

The main intersection in front of the school is packed. Cars honk. Yellow school buses idle, blowing hot exhaust.

Mr. Henderson, the school crossing guard, stands in the center of the street. He wears a bright orange vest and holds a red stop sign.

Aanya marches up to the curb. She has her notebook open.

AANYA
Mr. Henderson! The signal sequence is decaying.

MR. HENDERSON
(smiling)
Morning, Aanya! Keep moving onto the sidewalk, please. Keep the line flowing.

AANYA
The Maple Street light is still at four blinks. But Elm Street is down to two. Oak Street is down to one. By tomorrow morning at eight-fifteen, Maple Street will drop to zero blinks. It will go straight from walk to stop.

MR. HENDERSON
The lights are automatic, kiddo. The city computer handles all that. Now, inside the fence, please.

AANYA
But the computer has a virus! Or a broken timer! The children will walk when the cars are still moving!

Mr. Henderson waves a bus through. He does not look at her notebook.

MR. HENDERSON
Have a good day at school, Aanya!

EXT. BUSY INTERSECTION - AFTERNOON

Aanya walks home alone. The noise of the city is loud.

A motorcycle revs its engine. A construction drill starts up down the block.

The overlapping sounds begin to visually change. In Aanya's eyes, the sound waves become heavy, jagged neon-blue triangles that bounce off the concrete.

The flashing traffic lights turn into spinning red circles that expand and contract.

It is too much. The world is spinning.

Aanya does not scream. She does not run.

She pulls her gray noise-reducing headphones over her ears. The sharp blue triangles soften into round, fuzzy gray clouds.

She reaches into her pocket. Her fingers find the smooth gray pebble. She turns it over and over.

Her breathing slows down. One, two, three, four. She centers herself.

She looks at the crossing signal. It flashes once. Just once.

AANYA
(whispering)
The countdown is almost finished.

INT. AANYA'S BEDROOM - EVENING

Aanya's desk is covered in cardboard, wires, and small LED lights from her science kit.

She is building a model of the intersection. She connects a battery to a small dial.

Her mom, Priya, enters carrying a plate of sliced apples. Priya looks at the model with interest.

PRIYA
That looks complicated. Is this for the science fair?

AANYA
No. It is for tomorrow morning. I have to make them see. They cannot hear the numbers, so I have to show them the rhythm.

Priya sits on the edge of the bed. She does not call Aanya's project a waste of time.

PRIYA
You really believe the Maple Street light will fail tomorrow?

AANYA
I do not believe it. I know it. The math does not lie. It is a sequence.

Priya smiles warmly and places a hand on Aanya's shoulder.

PRIYA
Then I will make sure we get to school early tomorrow. We will show them together.

EXT. MAPLE STREET CROSSING - NEXT MORNING

The clock on the school wall reads 8:10 AM.

The street is a hive of activity. Parents are dropping off kids. Toby is talking to some friends near the steps.

Aanya stands at the curb. She has her cardboard model set up on top of a green trash can. The model has two flashing LEDs: one green, one red.

Beside her, Priya stands supportively, holding a flashlight to help illuminate Aanya's chart.

Officer Davis, the school transit officer, stands nearby. He is chatting with Mr. Henderson.

Aanya dials her model.

AANYA
Officer Davis! Mr. Henderson! Please look at this.

Officer Davis walks over, curious.

OFFICER DAVIS
What do we have here, young lady? A science project?

AANYA
This is the Maple Street crossing signal. Right now, it is safe. But in exactly four minutes, the decay pattern reaches this intersection.

She spins the dial on her model. The green light flashes, then instantly turns red without any warning blinks.

AANYA
The light will not blink to warn the walkers. It will just change.

OFFICER DAVIS
The city checked the main grid yesterday, kiddo. They said everything is green.

AANYA
They did not look at the pattern. They only looked at the current state. The sequence drops by one point two seconds every twelve hours. The twelve-hour mark is eight-fourteen.

Officer Davis looks at his watch. 8:12 AM.

OFFICER DAVIS
Well, that is very creative, but these big city systems do not just fail like—

Suddenly, the Maple Street pedestrian signal begins its cycle.

A group of children, including Toby, step toward the curb, ready to cross.

The white walking figure is lit.

Aanya watches her model. She counts down.

AANYA
Three. Two. One. Zero.

On the metal pole, the white walking figure vanishes.

There is no orange flashing hand. It does not blink at all.

It instantly turns into a solid red hand.

At that exact second, a delivery truck rounds the corner, speeding up to make the green light.

Toby steps off the curb.

TOBY
Hey!

Aanya jumps forward. She grabs Toby's arm and pulls him back onto the sidewalk.

The delivery truck roars past, its tires screeching as the driver slams on the brakes.

The truck stops inches from the crosswalk.

The crowd of parents and kids gasps.

Officer Davis stares at the signal. It is red. No warning blinks occurred.

OFFICER DAVIS
My goodness. It went straight to red.

Mr. Henderson runs over, his face pale.

MR. HENDERSON
She was right. The warning sequence is completely gone!

Officer Davis blows his whistle loudly. He steps into the street, stopping all traffic.

OFFICER DAVIS
Nobody cross! We are going manual!

He looks down at Aanya. He shakes his head in amazement.

OFFICER DAVIS
How did you know that was going to happen?

AANYA
I watched the other streets. They did the same thing. It is a sequence.

TOBY
She told you guys yesterday! Aanya is really good at patterns.

Officer Davis smiles and salutes Aanya.

OFFICER DAVIS
Well, thank you, officer. You saved us a major accident today. I am calling the city maintenance crew right now.

Priya wraps her arms around Aanya in a warm hug.

PRIYA
I am so proud of you, Aanya. You kept everyone safe.

Aanya smiles. She looks at her notebook, then crosses out the Maple Street line with a big, satisfying checkmark.

EXT. LIBRARY COURTYARD - AFTERNOON

The courtyard is completely quiet. The only sound is the gentle splash of a small stone fountain.

Aanya sits on the edge of the stone bench. Her mustard yellow sweater is cozy. Her noise-reducing headphones rest peacefully around her neck.

She is an autistic girl who loves the quiet.

She reaches into her pocket and pulls out the smooth gray pebble.

She places it carefully on the very corner of the bench, aligning it perfectly with the edge of the stone.

It fits. The pattern is complete.

She smiles, closes her eyes, and listens to the rhythmic splash of the water.

One. Two. Three. Four.
One. Two. Three. Four.

The sequence is perfect.